# The answer-choice step's refusals across every stored reading (D614), 2026-11-16, $0

`refusal_census.py` → `refusals.jsonl`, `refusal-census-output.txt`: every refusal of the frozen step in the stored gate-1 readings
(probe readings' control sends only).

- **174 refusals over 30 topics, N settled:** 137 justified, 24 split, 13 unjustified.
- **The 13 unjustified refusals** (both first readers, or both third readers) fall on 6 topics, all of one reason: "several figures and
  none is the main one":
  - custom accelerators' share of spending;
  - global semiconductor revenue with its AI part;
  - HBM shipment growth with the shortfall;
  - the hyperscalers' capex for 2026 and 2027;
  - the funded share of the equity commitments;
  - custom accelerator spending.
  In each case the readers name one figure as main and the rest as parts to list.
- **The 24 splits** add the same reason (the Intel stake, the Firmus investment) and "no listed theme covers it" (H100 and B200 rental
  prices, China's budget split). The Claude family fails these more often than the Codex family.
- **The gate-1 near-pass on Alex's 44** (D613) misses line 4 by three sends of this kind.

**This is the known class of D397, D469, D470, D555, D556 and D580** (`second-week/NO-MAIN-BOUNDARY-STUDY.md` §3):
- every rule tried in the answer-choice step either failed its guards or moved the error;
- the first-step route ("one figure for one year") was stopped after three attempts moved the error (D441, D465, D473, D477);
- the guards (the Intel stake and Firmus, which the readers say should never have been opened) are kept today by the refusal itself.

The study ends on a product choice no decision makes: when one matter carries two figures an analyst tracks apart, is it one topic with
no judged question (today), two topics, or one topic with two judged questions? Alex named the third as the gap when he chose the
first (D397).

**So the census quantifies a closed class and adds no candidate.**

