114-- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask
115-  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the
116-  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: "我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix").
117:- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,
118-  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a
119-  question, not something to build.
120-- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.
--
131-  word must catch the sentence it was written against and pass the corrected one.
132-- **Identity.** An identifier names one thing for the life of its scope; a changed thing gets a new id. Equal values alone never
133-  establish identity (D248 boundary 7, the same statement carried by several outlets, is a chosen exception, switched on only after its
134:  merge risk on the gold and the accuracy of the original speaker and day fields are measured).
135-- **Explicit inputs.** Library code takes runtime paths and configuration as arguments. A branch a run takes comes from its
136-  configuration, never from whether an environment variable happens to be set; a configured step whose credential is missing refuses
137-  the run. A component that sends a model call uses the transport its caller passes.
138-- **Never order by the wall clock.** It steps both ways; order comes from a sequence the program owns: dispatch's sequence number. The
139-  stream never reads the calendar; only pull mode acts on time, with its "now" passed in (D12, D13, D31).
140-- **Strictness is code too.** A check added to prevent defects can itself fail; accept it on real data, not samples.

